The testing symptoms dataframe has too many duplicate rows which causes misleading perfect scores.

Manual corruption of the testing symptoms must be done to impersonate the real-world noisy and unpredictable data.

This corrupted symptoms will clearly distinguish between the machines who just memorized the symptoms, from the machines who actually
learned the symptoms -> disease patterns.

The corruption leads to flipping off random cells of 0 into 1 and 1 into 0.


import numpy as np
from sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score
def corrupt(df, flip_frac=0.15, seed=42):
    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)
    mask = rng.random(df.shape) < flip_frac
    return df ^ mask.astype(int)

X_test_noisy = corrupt(wow)

for name, model in classifier_dictionary.items():
    acc_clean = accuracy_score(encoded_symbi_disease, model.predict(wow))
    acc_noisy = accuracy_score(encoded_symbi_disease, model.predict(X_test_noisy))
    print(name, acc_clean, acc_noisy, acc_clean - acc_noisy)
